# Link / Anchor

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A link takes someone to another page, another place on the same page or a file. A link goes somewhere. A button does something, such as turning on a lamp: for that, see [Button](buttons.html).

This page covers:

- Link / Anchor
  - `<a>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- The link text, which says where the link goes.
- The destination, in `href`.
- Optional notes in the text: that it opens a new tab, or that it is a file and how big.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-a"><code>&lt;a&gt;</code></h3>

The `<a>` element is a link when it has an `href`. Without `href` it is only a placeholder and is not a link.

- `href` is the address. `href="#heating"` jumps to the element with that `id` on the same page. Give that element `tabindex="-1"` so that focus can move to it.
- `target="_blank"` opens a new tab. Add `rel="noopener"` for older browsers.
- `download` saves the file.
- `hreflang` and `lang` say which language the destination or the link text is in.
- `rel="alternate"` marks a translation.
- Do not put a button or another link inside a link.
- Enter follows a link. Space does not.

```html
<p>To change when the heating comes on,
<a href="/local/heating-schedule-guide.html">read the heating schedule guide</a>.</p>
```

Never use a link as a button, for example `href="#"` with a script. Use a [Button](buttons.html).

**Link text.**

- Name the destination: “Read the heating schedule guide”, not “Click here” or “More”.
- Use the same text for the same destination, and different text for different destinations.
- Say in the text when a link opens a new tab or saves a file, and give the type and size: “Boiler manual (PDF, 2 MB, opens in a new tab)”.
- Do not use a long address as the link text.

**Where links appear in Home Assistant.**

- In a Markdown card, in dialogs and in help text you write. A link in a Markdown card takes `--primary-color`, or `--markdown-link-color` if a theme sets it. Each theme sets its own `primary-color`, so the link colour depends on the theme. In the Civica theme it is #1D70B8 in light mode and #C0D7EB in dark mode. For the Civica theme, calculated from those colours, that is about 5.2 to 1 on a white card and about 10 to 1 on the dark card surface. Check the other themes on your own dashboard.
- Each theme also sets `link-color` and `ha-color-text-link`, but only Civica’s own cards read them. A Markdown card does not. The light mode value is #1A65A6 in every theme; the dark mode value differs by theme, and is #C0D7EB in the Civica theme.
- Civica’s own [Action Links](action-links.html) card shows a list of links to dashboard paths, each at least 44 pixels high, with a Civica yellow focus background.
- A card’s tap action, such as `navigate` or `url`, is not a link. It is not a link element, so say in the card’s text where it goes.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.** The visited colour and the focus look of a link in a Markdown card are the browser’s and Home Assistant’s own. Check on your dashboard that links in a Markdown card are underlined, and add a cue other than colour if they are not.

For other special links, see [Skip link](skip-link.html), [Back Link](back-link.html), [Breadcrumb](breadcrumbs.html) and [Language Navigation](language-navigation.html).

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A link works without script and keeps browser features such as opening in a new tab.
- A link in a Markdown card follows `primary-color`, not Civica’s own link colour.
- A link’s focus look in a Markdown card cannot be set with the theme.
- Opening a new tab can surprise people, so say so in the text.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Write link text that makes sense out of context, and keep it the same as the heading of the page it opens. Do not rely on colour alone to show a link in a paragraph: use an underline. Keep stand-alone links at least 24 pixels high and apart, and 44 pixels in Civica’s own cards.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: a link in text needs a cue beyond colour.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) and 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: check link text on your own cards.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context): the text says where it goes.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): check the focus cue on your own dashboard.
- 2.5.3 Label in Name and 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): a link in a sentence is exempt, a stand-alone link needs 24 pixels.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: a link has a name from its text.

Specific text, one destination for each, and a note when a link leaves the page keep the effort low.

